All five keywords in ONE example:

```java
// ═══ ALL 5 KEYWORDS IN ONE BANKING SCENARIO ═══

// THROWS: declares that this method might throw (checked exception)
public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
    throws InsufficientFundsException {  // ← THROWS: warns caller!

    // THROW: creates and raises an exception
    if (amount.signum() <= 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive: " + amount);
        // ← THROW: raises exception NOW. Method stops here.
    }

    Connection conn = null;
    // TRY: code that might throw exceptions
    try {
        conn = dataSource.getConnection();
        debit(conn, from, amount);
        credit(conn, to, amount);
        conn.commit();
        return new TransferResult("SUCCESS", generateUTR());

    // CATCH: handle specific exceptions
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        rollback(conn);
        throw new TransferException("Transfer failed: " + from + "→" + to, e);
        // Catch SQL, wrap in domain exception, re-throw

    } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
        rollback(conn);
        throw e;  // re-throw — let caller handle business error

    // FINALLY: cleanup that ALWAYS runs
    } finally {
        if (conn != null) {
            try { conn.close(); } catch (SQLException e) { log.warn("Close failed", e); }
        }
        // Runs after try (success) OR catch (exception). ALWAYS.
    }
}

// Modern version with try-with-resources:
public TransferResult transferModern(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("positive required");

    try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {  // auto-close!
        debit(conn, from, amount);
        credit(conn, to, amount);
        conn.commit();
        return new TransferResult("SUCCESS", generateUTR());
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        throw new TransferException("Transfer failed", e);  // chain cause
    }
    // No finally needed! try-with-resources closes connection automatically.
}
```

WHY we need exception handling — without it:

```java
// ═══ WITHOUT exception handling: program CRASHES ═══
public void unsafeTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();  // might throw!
    debit(conn, from, amount);                      // might throw!
    credit(conn, to, amount);                       // might throw!
    conn.commit();                                   // might throw!
    conn.close();                                    // might throw!
    // If debit() succeeds but credit() fails:
    // → money DEBITED but NOT credited! LOST!
    // → connection NOT closed! LEAKED!
    // → no error message to customer!
    // → system crashes, other customers affected!
}

// ═══ WITH exception handling: graceful degradation ═══
public TransferResult safeTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
        debit(conn, from, amount);
        credit(conn, to, amount);
        conn.commit();
        return new TransferResult("SUCCESS");
    } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
        return new TransferResult("FAILED: " + e.getMessage());  // business error → inform customer
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        log.error("DB error during transfer", e);
        return new TransferResult("FAILED: system error, please retry");  // technical → graceful message
    }
    // Connection auto-closed. Transaction rolled back on exception. Customer gets clear response.
    // System continues serving other customers!
}
