sections.s2_technical:
Multi-catch lets a single catch block handle several exception types joined with the pipe symbol. It was added in Java 7 specifically to remove the duplicate code you get when two separate catch blocks do exactly the same thing.

```java
// ═══ SEPARATE catches with IDENTICAL bodies — duplication! ═══
try {
    byte[] raw = fileStore.read(accountId);   // throws IOException
    ledger.load(accountId);                   // throws SQLException
} catch (IOException e) {
    log.error("Export failed for {}", accountId, e);   // same body
    throw new ExportException("export failed", e);     // same body
} catch (SQLException e) {
    log.error("Export failed for {}", accountId, e);   // DUPLICATED!
    throw new ExportException("export failed", e);     // DUPLICATED!
}

// ═══ MULTI-CATCH — one block, no duplication ═══
try {
    byte[] raw = fileStore.read(accountId);   // throws IOException
    ledger.load(accountId);                   // throws SQLException
} catch (IOException | SQLException e) {       // ONE block handles both
    log.error("Export failed for {}", accountId, e);
    throw new ExportException("export failed", e);
}
```

The caught variable in a multi-catch is implicitly FINAL — you cannot reassign it. This is because the compiler cannot know which concrete type `e` holds, so it forbids reassignment to keep the type safe.

```java
// In MULTI-CATCH, e is implicitly final:
catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
    // e = new IOException("x");   // COMPILE ERROR — e is final here!
    log.error("Error: {}", e.getMessage());
}

// In a SINGLE catch, e is NOT final — you may reassign it:
catch (IOException e) {
    e = new IOException("wrapped");  // allowed in a single catch
    throw e;
}
```

The combined types must be UNRELATED. You cannot list a type and its parent together, because the parent already covers the child.

```java
// ILLEGAL — FileNotFoundException IS-A IOException (parent-child):
// catch (IOException | FileNotFoundException e) { }   // COMPILE ERROR

// LEGAL — IOException and SQLException are unrelated siblings:
catch (IOException | SQLException e) { }
```

The compiled bytecode for a multi-catch produces one handler entry that several exception-table rows point to, which is slightly more efficient than two separate handlers with identical bodies.

